---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../styles/museum.css";
---

<Layout
  title="The Jeep Incident | Museum of Dumb Ideas"
  description="The story of a 15-year-old, his first Jeep, and an early experiment in submersible vehicle testing."
>
  <div class="museum-page">
    <p class="museum-back">
      <a href="/museum/">← Museum of Dumb Ideas</a>
    </p>

    <header class="exhibit-header">
      <p class="exhibit-number">Accession No. MDI-0002</p>
      <h1>The Jeep Incident</h1>

      <p class="museum-subtitle">
        An early field study confirming that four-wheel drive does not include
        buoyancy.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="museum-plaque" aria-label="Exhibit information">
      <p class="plaque-label">Exhibit Information</p>

      <dl>
        <dt>Classification</dt>
        <dd>Early Submersible Vehicle Testing</dd>

        <dt>Year</dt>
        <dd>Circa 1980s</dd>

        <dt>Bad-Idea Consultant</dt>
        <dd>Donny</dd>

        <dt>Authorization</dt>
        <dd>Unanimous, two votes to zero</dd>

        <dt>Recovery Equipment</dt>
        <dd>Two wreckers and one tractor</dd>

        <dt>Primary Opponent</dt>
        <dd>Suction</dd>

        <dt>Parental Response</dt>
        <dd>Negative</dd>

        <dt>Historical Significance</dt>
        <dd>First recorded submarine adventure</dd>

        <dt>Lesson Learned</dt>
        <dd>Four-wheel drive does not include buoyancy</dd>
      </dl>
    </section>

    <article class="exhibit-body">
      <h2>Description</h2>

      <p>
        I was 15 when I bought my first vehicle, a Jeep, with money I had
        earned working at the Gulf Station and hauling hay around Van, Texas.
        There was one minor administrative problem: I did not yet have a
        driver's license.
      </p>

      <p>
        The oil roads became my unofficial transportation system. They offered
        miles of backroads, very little traffic, and a low chance of meeting
        anyone interested in reviewing my paperwork.
      </p>

      <p>
        Not long after I bought the Jeep, I took my brother-in-law Donny out to
        show it to him. Donny was several years older than me and an
        enthusiastic encourager of bad ideas. We were driving the backroads
        when he spotted a mud pit and suggested, in one form or another, that
        we should go through it. I agreed enthusiastically.
      </p>

      <p>
        This was not a puddle that had gotten ambitious. It was a legitimate
        mud pit.
      </p>

      <p>
        The Jeep had four-wheel drive, the mud was directly in front of us,
        and we had achieved unanimous approval.
      </p>

      <h2>Original Hypothesis</h2>

      <blockquote>
        The Jeep has four-wheel drive. It'll be fine.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>What Went Wrong</h2>

      <p>
        The Jeep went in. It did not come back out.
      </p>

      <p>
        Forward progress ended, the Jeep settled into the mud, and suction
        took over. Pulling it out eventually required two wreckers and a
        tractor. The recovery felt less like towing a vehicle and more like
        extracting an archaeological discovery.
      </p>

      <p>
        My parents were not pleased. A few years later, I joined the Navy
        submarine force. In retrospect, this was my first submarine adventure.
      </p>

      <h2>Photographic Evidence</h2>

      <div class="artifact-gallery">
        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/museum/jeep-stuck-recovery-underway.jpeg"
            alt="Brad and Donny standing beside a Jeep buried to its axles in a muddy clearing"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            Brad and Donny assess the Jeep after the mud took control of the
            project.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="/images/museum/brad-posing-with-stuck-jeep.jpeg"
            alt="Fifteen-year-old Brad posing on the hood of the mud-covered Jeep"
            loading="lazy"
          />
          <figcaption>
            The curator at 15, presenting the results with appropriate
            confidence.
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>

      <h2>Artifacts</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Two surviving photographs.</li>
        <li>One thoroughly buried Jeep.</li>
        <li>Two wreckers and one tractor.</li>
        <li>An early contribution to submarine research.</li>
      </ul>

      <aside class="curator-note">
        <h2>Curator's Note</h2>

        <p>
          Buying the Jeep was the responsible part. The unofficial oil-road
          transportation system and Donny's external consulting created the
          conditions for the exhibit.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <h2>Exhibit Rating</h2>

      <div class="rating-grid">
        <article class="rating-card">
          <h3>Confidence</h3>
          <p class="rating-value" aria-label="Five out of five stars">
            ★★★★★
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rating-card">
          <h3>Planning</h3>
          <p class="rating-value" aria-label="One out of five stars">
            ★☆☆☆☆
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rating-card">
          <h3>Traction</h3>
          <p class="rating-value" aria-label="One out of five stars">
            ★☆☆☆☆
          </p>
        </article>

        <article class="rating-card">
          <h3>Storytelling Value</h3>
          <p class="rating-value" aria-label="Five out of five stars">
            ★★★★★
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <h2>Lessons Learned</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Confidence is not traction.</li>
        <li>Four-wheel drive does not include buoyancy.</li>
        <li>Unanimous approval is not the same thing as a good idea.</li>
      </ul>

      <footer class="museum-footer">
        <p>
          The Jeep Incident is on permanent loan from the Archives of
          Adolescent Confidence.
        </p>

        <p>
          <a href="/museum/">Continue through the Museum</a>
          ·
          <a href="/">Return to BradCooke.com</a>
        </p>
      </footer>
    </article>
  </div>
</Layout>
